1
00:00:05,086 --> 00:00:16,380
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering about the responsibility again, of being clear about what one is responsible for.

2
00:00:21,131 --> 00:00:39,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you responsible for the... Well, I'm saying that you're not responsible for the arising of the possibilities for acting out of greed, lust or delusion.

3
00:00:39,344 --> 00:00:44,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's always a possibility that you can act out of lust.

4
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:51,670
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not responsible for that arising of that possibility.

5
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:52,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

6
00:00:52,871 --> 00:00:54,633
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that's on the level of Mara.

7
00:00:55,534 --> 00:00:57,556
[SPEAKER_01]: When Mara throws these options at you.

8
00:00:57,796 --> 00:00:59,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, why don't you do this?

9
00:00:59,217 --> 00:01:00,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, hey, look at that.

10
00:01:00,838 --> 00:01:02,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't you... go over there.

11
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:06,323
[SPEAKER_01]: But you choosing to accept and act, that's on you.

12
00:01:06,944 --> 00:01:08,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara cannot make you do that.

13
00:01:11,205 --> 00:01:21,833
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you will never even... well, you're never gonna see where the line for your responsibility is, if by default, you're refusing to accept it, even for the obvious... obvious things.

14
00:01:25,038 --> 00:01:34,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, by accepting the possibility that, oh, I can go and have sexual intercourse, therefore I must.

15
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:35,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

16
00:01:35,423 --> 00:01:37,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, hey, you know, that's just...

17
00:01:37,645 --> 00:01:43,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the only way for you to not do that, is to ignore the possibility is there.

18
00:01:43,848 --> 00:01:49,090
[SPEAKER_00]: I must think about these possibilities, because I will be unable to restrain myself.

19
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:49,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

20
00:01:50,111 --> 00:01:52,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you have to do that in the beginning, perhaps.

21
00:01:55,742 --> 00:02:00,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Until you sort of increase mental strength.

22
00:02:01,305 --> 00:02:07,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to... you have to recognize that you must deal with it eventually.

23
00:02:07,368 --> 00:02:09,709
[SPEAKER_01]: With the fact that these possibilities will always remain.

24
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:11,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And they were never the problem.

25
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:15,052
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem was that you could not help but act out of it.

26
00:02:15,392 --> 00:02:22,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I just can't... restrain from saying such something when I get irritated.

27
00:02:23,475 --> 00:02:24,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, actually, you can.

28
00:02:24,496 --> 00:02:27,178
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you can't is you keep choosing to say it.

29
00:02:28,478 --> 00:02:35,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start recognizing that it's not about not having an option to say something or not having an option to do something.

30
00:02:36,484 --> 00:02:42,368
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not being able to resist that pressure of saying something or doing something.

31
00:02:42,428 --> 00:02:43,088
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

32
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:44,409
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem, yeah, yeah.

33
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:52,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't know... Well, if you never look at, like, say, an option to say something out of irritation, you never stop and look at it.

34
00:02:54,201 --> 00:03:00,742
[SPEAKER_01]: You either just say it, or try to remove the option, and thus, I will remove the pressure of having to say something.

35
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:02,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that's overstepping.

36
00:03:03,603 --> 00:03:13,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're again going into Māra's domain, either through overly accepting what he has to offer you as an option, or overly denying and trying to tell him not to offer you that as an option, because you can't help yourself.

37
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:16,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, please, don't even tempt me.

38
00:03:16,086 --> 00:03:17,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's not on you to decide.

39
00:03:19,206 --> 00:03:21,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Temptations are there, because you keep giving in to them.

40
00:03:23,578 --> 00:03:27,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Temptations are a problem, because you keep giving in to them.

41
00:03:27,221 --> 00:03:28,041
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep choosing them.

42
00:03:28,362 --> 00:03:32,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you recognize that temptations are the problem, but you can't stop choosing them.

43
00:03:33,125 --> 00:03:38,248
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you try to prevent Mahārāja from tempting you, which means you react to his temptations again.

44
00:03:39,509 --> 00:03:42,271
[SPEAKER_01]: You made a problem out of that, which it isn't.

45
00:03:42,291 --> 00:03:42,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

46
00:03:43,012 --> 00:03:46,054
[SPEAKER_01]: If you accept that you... if you're...

47
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:53,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Rule of thumb is to accept responsibility for your choices, even if you're unclear about it.

48
00:03:54,342 --> 00:03:58,324
[SPEAKER_01]: You lean on to, well, it's probably on me, not because of this or this.

49
00:03:58,824 --> 00:04:04,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You will stop blaming circumstances, including other people or whatever else, for your choices.

50
00:04:05,326 --> 00:04:12,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will also stop blaming Mara for presenting you with those choices, or presenting you with limited, because sometimes people complain they don't have enough choices.

51
00:04:13,604 --> 00:04:16,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, they're not responsible for what they choose on account of limited choices.

52
00:04:17,047 --> 00:04:17,207
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

53
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:18,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice is a choice.

54
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:21,311
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, you're responsible for choosing it.

55
00:04:21,391 --> 00:04:28,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter whether the content of that choice is great or limited, fortunate, unfortunate.

56
00:04:28,238 --> 00:04:28,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

57
00:04:29,559 --> 00:04:31,041
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is that you choose it.

58
00:04:32,302 --> 00:04:34,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, circumstances are different for different people.

59
00:04:34,764 --> 00:04:35,545
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it works.

60
00:04:37,392 --> 00:04:46,515
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is that, regardless of the circumstances, whether they're favorable choices, agreeable choices, disagreeable, limited choices, I only had offers of bad choices.

61
00:04:47,656 --> 00:04:50,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, responsibility for choosing is a new thing.

62
00:04:51,597 --> 00:04:53,218
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not absorbed by responsibility.

63
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:57,039
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how bad choices Māra have presented you with.

64
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:02,741
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the nature of existence.

65
00:05:03,341 --> 00:05:05,162
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you feel justified,

66
00:05:06,213 --> 00:05:18,063
[SPEAKER_01]: In what you've chosen, because the choices were limited, your choices were bad, your choices were not as good as for other people, you're ignoring where responsibility is, and that's why you are basically trapped by Māra.

67
00:05:19,633 --> 00:05:21,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because choices were bad or choices were good.

68
00:05:22,013 --> 00:05:28,895
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you don't see where the line is, so you wander, as the Buddha would say, into the the hunter's trap, not knowing that that's a trap.

69
00:05:29,335 --> 00:05:30,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing this is the line.

70
00:05:30,475 --> 00:05:31,675
[SPEAKER_01]: If I cross, I'll get trapped.

71
00:05:32,215 --> 00:05:32,855
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the noose.

72
00:05:33,495 --> 00:05:36,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I step out, if I stay outside of it, he can't trap me.

73
00:05:36,596 --> 00:05:37,356
[SPEAKER_01]: I can go around it.

74
00:05:38,436 --> 00:05:38,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

75
00:05:38,656 --> 00:05:41,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the entry point is your choice.

76
00:05:41,937 --> 00:05:45,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring that you're responsible for your choice, regardless of the circumstances.

77
00:05:45,657 --> 00:05:49,058
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a hard... For many people, it's a hard pill to swallow, because

78
00:05:50,545 --> 00:06:03,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's not like easier, it's not consciously done, but it's kind of natural response of an ignorant mind is to, oh, well, I've chosen that because of this or because of that.

79
00:06:04,270 --> 00:06:11,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because, you know, and also you perceive difference in people's choices, difference in people's circumstances.

80
00:06:11,975 --> 00:06:17,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's the whole, if only I had these options, I would not have chosen what I've chosen, I would have chosen better and so on.

81
00:06:18,718 --> 00:06:20,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's probably true, but it's beside the point.

82
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:27,585
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, doesn't matter what options there are, doesn't matter what choices you made, you will be responsible for your choices.

83
00:06:27,785 --> 00:06:30,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the, like, the... the heavy weight of that recognition...

84
00:06:30,988 --> 00:06:31,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice that you make, yeah.

85
00:06:31,968 --> 00:06:32,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice that you make, yeah.

86
00:06:33,309 --> 00:06:37,152
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the heavy weight of the recognition of beings are owners of that kāma.

87
00:06:37,192 --> 00:06:38,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Kāma, literally, is a choice.

88
00:06:39,354 --> 00:06:41,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And an action that comes out of the choice.

89
00:06:41,416 --> 00:06:42,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your choice is already an action.

90
00:06:44,379 --> 00:06:51,926
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, ah, that's what the Buddha meant by, like, really contemplate that, because it's only on you and you will be bound with the results of it.

91
00:06:52,086 --> 00:06:57,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what choices you had, what say... that's all pre- you acting on account of the choices.

92
00:06:58,152 --> 00:06:59,813
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you do, that's on you.

93
00:07:01,395 --> 00:07:05,598
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, there is... there's never an excuse to act out of greed, aversion, delusion.

94
00:07:05,679 --> 00:07:09,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what circumstances you were presented with to act upon.

95
00:07:12,682 --> 00:07:16,525
[SPEAKER_00]: These were my most pressing choices, so to speak.

96
00:07:17,906 --> 00:07:25,331
[SPEAKER_01]: And another thing is, like, that's why you cannot just step outside of that khamma, that basically being being condemned to choose.

97
00:07:26,231 --> 00:07:31,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot step outside of it simply by choosing this on account of this, because you still are bound to choice.

98
00:07:32,068 --> 00:07:34,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can choose better, you can choose worse.

99
00:07:35,010 --> 00:07:38,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Choosing non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion is better than greed, aversion, delusion.

100
00:07:38,673 --> 00:07:42,295
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, it still keeps you bound to that pressure of choice.

101
00:07:43,556 --> 00:07:47,039
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point of, like, you cannot possibly not choose.

102
00:07:47,539 --> 00:07:50,181
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, I will not choose any of this, you choose that.

103
00:07:50,581 --> 00:07:53,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And that might be the right choice, if all the other choices are bad.

104
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:56,211
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, it's within the choice.

105
00:07:56,351 --> 00:08:07,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, you cannot... in order to step outside of... to basically free yourself from karma and pressure of it and saṃsāra and everything, all the implications of your choices and results, you need to become an arahant.

106
00:08:08,498 --> 00:08:17,104
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to fully uproot the slightest craving, the slightest holding onto things and so on, in order to then truly be free from that burden of choice.

107
00:08:17,544 --> 00:08:18,985
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can do it by choosing.

108
00:08:19,790 --> 00:08:26,512
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessary basis for developing that is to choose non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, regardless of the circumstances.

109
00:08:26,552 --> 00:08:33,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, there is never like, oh, well, see, I didn't have the fortunate options to choose, so I had to choose greed, aversion, delusion.

110
00:08:33,935 --> 00:08:34,075
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

111
00:08:34,335 --> 00:08:41,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That's choice of greed, aversion, delusion, or choice of non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion is always internal, regardless of the circumstances.

112
00:08:45,825 --> 00:08:53,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, if you avoid... if you're ignoring responsibility of that internally, you will, by default, project the same attitude externally.

113
00:08:54,159 --> 00:08:56,562
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, everything will be everyone else's fault.

114
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:06,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the fault of the impersonal, oh, if only I've lived in a better culture, or if only I live in a better state, or in a better society, or a better family.

115
00:09:06,647 --> 00:09:07,488
[SPEAKER_01]: It's never on you.

116
00:09:07,848 --> 00:09:11,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Because internally, you're unable to accept it.

117
00:09:11,391 --> 00:09:14,213
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of it, you just keep mirroring the same attitude elsewhere.

118
00:09:14,233 --> 00:09:17,695
[SPEAKER_00]: But it should be actually quite a liberating insight.

119
00:09:17,936 --> 00:09:20,477
[SPEAKER_01]: After you deal with the fright of it,

120
00:09:22,759 --> 00:09:30,742
[SPEAKER_01]: It's liberating, yes, but you need to be basically courageous enough to accept that weight, anxiety of responsibility.

121
00:09:31,023 --> 00:09:33,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, things are not in my control.

122
00:09:34,184 --> 00:09:36,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Options that are presented to you are not in your control.

123
00:09:36,845 --> 00:09:39,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, if I choose them, it's on me.

124
00:09:39,746 --> 00:09:41,347
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't choose them, it's on me.

125
00:09:42,352 --> 00:09:44,934
[SPEAKER_01]: If I abstain from choice, well, that's another choice.

126
00:09:44,994 --> 00:09:46,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Postponing the choice is what I choose.

127
00:09:47,597 --> 00:09:48,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is on me.

128
00:09:48,718 --> 00:09:52,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, oh my goodness, beings are owner of their actions.

129
00:09:52,822 --> 00:09:54,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when people would recognize that in the suttas.

130
00:09:55,524 --> 00:09:57,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this is inescapable.

131
00:09:57,526 --> 00:10:02,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I go, or stay back, or both, or neither, I am under it.

132
00:10:04,112 --> 00:10:05,313
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I escape this?

133
00:10:06,033 --> 00:10:08,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the Buddhist teaching comes into play.

134
00:10:08,354 --> 00:10:14,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you're saying, there can be initial fear, like, whoa, I'm responsible, but then, I'm responsible.

135
00:10:15,959 --> 00:10:18,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, oh, so you're not subjected to chance anymore.

136
00:10:19,001 --> 00:10:25,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, your well-being is not dependent on chance anymore, because you recognize well-being is basically on the level of that

137
00:10:26,767 --> 00:10:28,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, emotional background of your choices.

138
00:10:29,269 --> 00:10:29,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

139
00:10:29,729 --> 00:10:35,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're responsible for your choices, then, ah, you will be responsible for the result of those choices, which is that emotional background.

140
00:10:36,174 --> 00:10:45,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha would say the... a man who is... who is not... who is sort of, um, recognizes, oh, I should act for my own welfare and welfare of others.

141
00:10:46,301 --> 00:10:49,864
[SPEAKER_01]: He's the one who will not neglect that, because you realize that's what welfare comes from.

142
00:10:50,842 --> 00:10:55,384
[SPEAKER_01]: My well-being, my welfare, is by not choosing things upon greed, aversion, delusion.

143
00:10:55,404 --> 00:10:57,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to approve that.

144
00:10:57,204 --> 00:10:57,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

145
00:10:57,865 --> 00:11:10,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you stick to it, and if you don't develop greater wisdom, you're still going to have greater welfare, internally, than if you just blindly choose and follow aimlessly towards whatever's thrown at you.

146
00:11:10,729 --> 00:11:11,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want this, I don't want that.

147
00:11:12,230 --> 00:11:14,131
[SPEAKER_01]: And even then, by the way, you have a criteria.

148
00:11:14,191 --> 00:11:15,651
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to be the criteria.

149
00:11:16,472 --> 00:11:19,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You can start questioning as to, why do I always choose pleasure?

150
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:22,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I always choose to resist pain?

151
00:11:23,542 --> 00:11:24,943
[SPEAKER_01]: There must be some reason for that.

152
00:11:26,764 --> 00:11:27,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's natural.

153
00:11:27,524 --> 00:11:29,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what is the natural reason for it?

154
00:11:29,926 --> 00:11:32,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's basically how I feel.

155
00:11:32,907 --> 00:11:47,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my... the sole governing power of my whole existence, my values, my actions, the results of it, everything else I have to bear on account of it, is simply my feelings, how I feel at the time.

156
00:11:47,817 --> 00:11:49,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Could I perhaps say no to how I feel?

157
00:11:49,878 --> 00:11:50,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

158
00:11:50,927 --> 00:11:55,650
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say no to the reasoned feeling, but you can say no to acting out of it, because that's another option, see?

159
00:11:57,071 --> 00:11:59,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to act towards pleasure.

160
00:11:59,752 --> 00:12:02,374
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to act away from pain, but that takes training.

161
00:12:03,454 --> 00:12:08,217
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why with the grain of an ignorant mind and against the grain of the mind that devoured wisdom.

162
00:12:12,446 --> 00:12:19,151
[SPEAKER_00]: But the result, if you're going in that right direction, can only be the right result, the best result.

163
00:12:19,251 --> 00:12:22,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Can only be the result of going in the right direction.

164
00:12:22,453 --> 00:12:23,374
[SPEAKER_01]: A beneficial result.

165
00:12:23,494 --> 00:12:23,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:12:23,854 --> 00:12:26,636
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what literally kusala means.

167
00:12:26,716 --> 00:12:27,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It means beneficial.

168
00:12:28,617 --> 00:12:31,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Akusala is non-beneficial.

169
00:12:31,818 --> 00:12:33,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, un-beneficial.

170
00:12:33,619 --> 00:12:34,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Un-beneficial, yeah.

171
00:12:34,699 --> 00:12:36,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And kusala means beneficial.

172
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:40,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Beneficial for you and by proxy then for everybody else.

173
00:12:41,121 --> 00:12:45,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if your mind is free from greed, aversion, delusion, you cannot possibly be perpetuating that towards others.

174
00:12:47,583 --> 00:12:47,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

175
00:12:48,632 --> 00:12:50,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Or instigating it in others.

176
00:12:50,713 --> 00:12:59,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a win-win for everyone, if everybody would individually start accepting responsibility, not just for things they do, but things they contemplate doing.

177
00:13:01,059 --> 00:13:07,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for your own, basically, action by mind, speech and the body, for your choices.

178
00:13:07,923 --> 00:13:11,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you choose something, it's not like, okay, so I will not choose.

179
00:13:11,986 --> 00:13:12,686
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

180
00:13:12,866 --> 00:13:13,447
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your choice.

181
00:13:14,367 --> 00:13:21,753
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to know the reason behind your choices, means, basically, that's how you prevent obscuring your responsibility behind your choices.

182
00:13:23,774 --> 00:13:29,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is chosen, and I mean, like, oh, I've chosen this because he said such and such, so I've done this.

183
00:13:29,258 --> 00:13:29,579
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

184
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:35,003
[SPEAKER_01]: He said such and such, and I chose that to be the reason for me acting like this.

185
00:13:37,959 --> 00:13:42,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how did I act then, on account of me choosing that to be the reason for my action?

186
00:13:42,987 --> 00:13:46,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I acted with greed, I acted with aversion, there's no justification for that.

187
00:13:47,442 --> 00:13:56,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you remove greed, aversion, delusion as a motivation for your action, then you can say, no, he said that, and I acted like this, without greed, without aversion, without delusion.

188
00:13:56,610 --> 00:14:00,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not blameworthy for acting in this manner and not acting in that manner.

189
00:14:00,994 --> 00:14:04,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Certain things, yes, killing, stealing, cheating, lying, that's always blameworthy.

190
00:14:04,998 --> 00:14:11,224
[SPEAKER_01]: But other things are blameworthy if rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, and not blameworthy if they're not rooted in those very things.

191
00:14:13,743 --> 00:14:15,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no like, I must always act like this.

192
00:14:15,864 --> 00:14:16,044
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

193
00:14:16,224 --> 00:14:18,486
[SPEAKER_01]: I must always not act out of greed, aversion, delusion.

194
00:14:18,626 --> 00:14:21,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter particularly what I might do on account of it.

195
00:14:22,008 --> 00:14:23,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I did not speak softly enough.

196
00:14:23,709 --> 00:14:24,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

197
00:14:25,850 --> 00:14:28,331
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't... Was it rooted in greed, aversion, delusion?

198
00:14:28,532 --> 00:14:28,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

199
00:14:29,012 --> 00:14:29,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it matters.

200
00:14:29,852 --> 00:14:31,613
[SPEAKER_01]: But not because whether you've spoken softly or not.

201
00:14:31,994 --> 00:14:39,538
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it was rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, irritation, lust, ignoring, basically, indolence, and so on.

202
00:14:42,299 --> 00:15:06,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you do that long enough, that training, not acting out of greed, hatred, delusion, then, if you get used to that, or you're incapable of acting out of greed, hatred, delusion, you still have the options always there, presented by Mara.

203
00:15:08,885 --> 00:15:09,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

204
00:15:10,812 --> 00:15:12,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're no longer pressured by it.

205
00:15:13,514 --> 00:15:14,795
[SPEAKER_01]: For a period of time.

206
00:15:16,917 --> 00:15:25,783
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, Māra will keep presenting you with options, for as long as there is a chance that you will choose them, that you will act out upon them.

207
00:15:26,544 --> 00:15:32,909
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have been steadfast long enough, and that there is no set time, this is how long it has to be.

208
00:15:33,549 --> 00:15:34,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends on people.

209
00:15:37,146 --> 00:15:40,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Individually, it's determined how long you need to be steadfast.

210
00:15:40,109 --> 00:15:43,552
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you are steadfast long enough, Māra will stop.

211
00:15:44,253 --> 00:15:44,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Māra will leave you.

212
00:15:45,394 --> 00:15:47,556
[SPEAKER_01]: He'll stop even presenting, entertaining the obstacles.

213
00:15:47,576 --> 00:15:56,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when the Buddha says in the suttas, after his enlightenment, Māra followed him for seven years, trying to find an opening, like a crow trying to get something to bite on.

214
00:15:57,525 --> 00:16:01,426
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you gave up, because you realized, oh, this is... this is steadfast.

215
00:16:01,446 --> 00:16:06,488
[SPEAKER_01]: There is absolutely no coming back towards that which I can, you know, bait people with, and so on.

216
00:16:06,548 --> 00:16:07,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you left it.

217
00:16:11,269 --> 00:16:11,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

218
00:16:11,789 --> 00:16:20,012
[SPEAKER_01]: These... and there is a similar sutta, which says like, the flies will be sort of flying around, where they can still perceive something worth landing on.

219
00:16:21,282 --> 00:16:29,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But even when everything is cleaned, all the impurities are gone, the flies will still be around, because there's a habit of hanging around and landing on something.

220
00:16:29,428 --> 00:16:33,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Until, long enough, there hasn't been anything offered for them to land on, and they'll leave.

221
00:16:34,072 --> 00:16:34,852
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll find somebody else.

222
00:16:36,153 --> 00:16:36,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

223
00:16:36,634 --> 00:16:39,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say, the arahant,

224
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:57,721
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't kill anyone, but if there's a gun and there's someone, some criminal attacking someone else, that option of taking a gun and shooting a criminal, for example, just... Well, as a thought, I suppose, it's there.

225
00:16:57,941 --> 00:16:59,362
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a possibility in the world.

226
00:17:00,003 --> 00:17:01,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But for him...

227
00:17:01,124 --> 00:17:02,004
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

228
00:17:02,024 --> 00:17:03,145
[SPEAKER_00]: ...to choose that, he can't...

229
00:17:03,165 --> 00:17:03,705
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

230
00:17:03,745 --> 00:17:06,007
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't be pressured in any way to choose that.

231
00:17:06,027 --> 00:17:06,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

232
00:17:06,347 --> 00:17:07,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean, that...

233
00:17:09,029 --> 00:17:13,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Those possibilities... Yeah, but there are no possibilities in the world independent of individuals.

234
00:17:15,153 --> 00:17:17,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Every possibility is on the base of individual experience.

235
00:17:17,855 --> 00:17:22,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, talking abstractly about possibilities in the world, it's only applicable based on the individual.

236
00:17:23,739 --> 00:17:25,540
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you can't talk abstractly about it.

237
00:17:26,260 --> 00:17:28,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is the person free from greed, aversion, delusion?

238
00:17:29,337 --> 00:17:33,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Motion of picking up the gun and shooting somebody, killing them, is a possibility.

239
00:17:33,358 --> 00:17:36,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're saying the meaning of those things have gone, or gone.

240
00:17:36,118 --> 00:17:38,319
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessary basis for those things is removed.

241
00:17:38,339 --> 00:17:44,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha would make those declarations like, uh... Arhant is incapable of...

242
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:50,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Such thing is impossible to occur in universe of all the eons and realms.

243
00:17:50,921 --> 00:17:53,242
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for such and such to occur.

244
00:17:54,022 --> 00:17:58,384
[SPEAKER_01]: For Sotāpanna, it's impossible to take anybody else as a teacher.

245
00:17:58,424 --> 00:17:59,124
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

246
00:17:59,804 --> 00:18:07,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you still think abstractly of these possibilities, independent of individuals, you might think, well, how is that impossible for him to take somebody else's teaching?

247
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:15,046
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize there is no such thing as abstract possibilities independent of individuals, there has to be a basis.

248
00:18:15,167 --> 00:18:16,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance or non-ignorance.

249
00:18:16,288 --> 00:18:17,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion or non-aversion.

250
00:18:17,929 --> 00:18:18,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust or non-lust.

251
00:18:19,491 --> 00:18:20,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And person has uprooted that.

252
00:18:21,353 --> 00:18:25,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's impossible for anybody else, for such person to follow any different teacher.

253
00:18:25,858 --> 00:18:29,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, certain things require certain basis of greed, aversion, ignorance.

254
00:18:31,343 --> 00:18:33,566
[SPEAKER_01]: to be acted upon, to be maintained.

255
00:18:33,806 --> 00:18:40,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Once these things are gone, it's inconceivable for those things to be done, which means that's when those possibilities will cease for those people.

256
00:18:40,754 --> 00:18:45,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no possibility for an arhant to commit murder, or sexual intercourse, for that matter.

257
00:18:45,460 --> 00:18:46,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

258
00:18:47,253 --> 00:18:51,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the meaning of that object and... Well, that's the thing.

259
00:18:51,776 --> 00:18:58,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Objects are known through the meaning, but usually, yes, people would kind of get the meaning on account of the objects.

260
00:18:58,922 --> 00:19:06,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have fully understood the nature of it, you realize the meaning is that which determines it, and that's how objects are known as objects.

261
00:19:06,988 --> 00:19:07,548
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a gun.

262
00:19:08,349 --> 00:19:09,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, a meaning like...

263
00:19:09,610 --> 00:19:13,573
[SPEAKER_01]: He might use a gun to, you know, hammer a nail or something, but he better put a safety on.

264
00:19:15,789 --> 00:19:20,132
[SPEAKER_01]: He will not have the... like, he can't even have a memory.

265
00:19:20,312 --> 00:19:25,395
[SPEAKER_01]: If he used to shoot people for whatever reason, like Angulimala, he didn't have a gun, but say, he did.

266
00:19:25,655 --> 00:19:26,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no.

267
00:19:26,576 --> 00:19:36,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has a memory of using this to commit murder, but the significance, the context of it, the meaning of killing, it's impossible for him to do that.

268
00:19:39,123 --> 00:19:41,645
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, again, it's all determined by the intention.

269
00:19:42,293 --> 00:19:49,876
[SPEAKER_01]: There were those cases in the suttas when a monk accidentally sat on those... like a big seat with many cushions, and there's a baby underneath, sleeping.

270
00:19:50,716 --> 00:19:53,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And he killed it.

271
00:19:53,057 --> 00:19:54,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely unintentional.

272
00:19:54,418 --> 00:20:01,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha made the rule, you need to check, lift up things, basically, before you sit down.

273
00:20:01,921 --> 00:20:03,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that was his fault.

274
00:20:03,561 --> 00:20:04,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Not killing.

275
00:20:05,142 --> 00:20:09,764
[SPEAKER_01]: His fault was not checking, so he was carelessly just threw himself back on this seat.

276
00:20:10,484 --> 00:20:10,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:20:12,509 --> 00:20:13,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And he might have well been an arahant.

278
00:20:15,591 --> 00:20:16,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not killing.

279
00:20:16,551 --> 00:20:20,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Killing is determined by the intention to take away the life.

280
00:20:21,053 --> 00:20:21,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

281
00:20:21,934 --> 00:20:24,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that intention is impossible for him.

282
00:20:26,096 --> 00:20:34,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you cannot have that intention without a degree of aversion or lust or ignorance, which he has completely uprooted.

283
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:46,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why that kind of response, mundane sense of responsibility, is the place to start and never abandon it.

284
00:20:46,102 --> 00:20:47,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Just cultivate it more and more.

285
00:20:48,363 --> 00:20:55,725
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to get beyond that fear and into the recognition that this is actually, this is a great position.

286
00:20:56,010 --> 00:21:12,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because once people start recognizing that responsibility, anxiety comes, and then you can sort of get like a choice paralysis, so to speak, like, whatever I do, but then you realize, well, that is your choice now as well, abstaining from these choices because you can't bear the weight of it.

287
00:21:12,725 --> 00:21:19,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, you have to actually accept it and endure it, endure the unpleasantness of it, and recognize that that's your choice as well.

288
00:21:20,933 --> 00:21:21,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But get the results.

289
00:21:22,602 --> 00:21:25,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you persist, you will have to get the results.

290
00:21:26,084 --> 00:21:31,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order to keep ignoring responsibility, that attitude, you need to keep maintaining it.

291
00:21:31,968 --> 00:21:34,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep acting out of ignoring responsibility.

292
00:21:35,051 --> 00:21:37,612
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep maintaining the views that you are not responsible.

293
00:21:37,652 --> 00:21:40,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It's someone else's fault, circumstances, lack of fortune.

294
00:21:41,315 --> 00:21:44,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Or when people say, oh, you know, maybe in the next life and I'm not...

295
00:21:45,118 --> 00:21:46,639
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have enough pāramīs in this life.

296
00:21:47,179 --> 00:21:49,401
[SPEAKER_01]: How could you possibly know that?

297
00:21:49,421 --> 00:21:51,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You just decided that as an excuse.

298
00:21:52,035 --> 00:21:57,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you chose that to be an excuse for abstaining from... for avoiding responsibility of your own choices.

299
00:21:58,779 --> 00:22:06,964
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you're responsible for basically keeping yourself in the state of non-responsibility, which is greed, aversion, delusion.

300
00:22:17,505 --> 00:22:29,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, you cannot possibly be accepting, cultivating sense responsibility without mindfulness, without composure, without, you know, carefully attending to what you're choosing, what you're thinking, what you're going to say.

301
00:22:33,719 --> 00:22:35,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Being mindful again.

302
00:22:35,501 --> 00:22:36,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Responsibility.

303
00:22:37,363 --> 00:22:39,324
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're responsible, you're mindful.

304
00:22:39,825 --> 00:22:42,167
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're mindful, you'll be aware of responsibility.

305
00:22:44,583 --> 00:22:45,984
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you have no excuses.

306
00:22:46,505 --> 00:22:48,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Because an excuse, you're responsible for it.

307
00:22:51,809 --> 00:22:55,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant is the only being, you could call it, that is excuse-free.

308
00:22:56,293 --> 00:22:56,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely.

309
00:22:56,793 --> 00:22:57,213
[SPEAKER_01]: He's free.

310
00:22:57,754 --> 00:22:58,715
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't need any excuse.

311
00:22:58,755 --> 00:23:01,757
[SPEAKER_01]: He's completely free from any blame, any excuses.

312
00:23:02,878 --> 00:23:04,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh... Actually, we called that, didn't we?

313
00:23:04,699 --> 00:23:05,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Blameless.

314
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:05,760
[SPEAKER_01]: The blameless one.

315
00:23:06,681 --> 00:23:09,683
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing there that can be a basis for blame.

316
00:23:10,264 --> 00:23:11,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean people won't blame him.

317
00:23:11,745 --> 00:23:13,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Because ignorant people, obviously, perceive

318
00:23:14,604 --> 00:23:16,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Things to be blamed which are not blameworthy in themselves.

319
00:23:18,727 --> 00:23:22,530
[SPEAKER_01]: But internally speaking, which is the only measure, he is blameless.

320
00:23:23,090 --> 00:23:25,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are... it's excuse-free.

321
00:23:25,652 --> 00:23:32,457
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs no excuses for anything.

322
00:23:33,998 --> 00:23:35,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It simplifies things as well.

323
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:36,760
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yes.

324
00:23:36,840 --> 00:23:40,063
[SPEAKER_01]: But if... like, you know, the simple context doesn't mean it's easy.

325
00:23:40,743 --> 00:23:41,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it's maybe, you know,

326
00:23:43,966 --> 00:23:58,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Less sort of proliferated, maybe it's less defined, less elaborated on, which kind of, for some people, might make it even harder to grasp.

327
00:24:00,313 --> 00:24:09,197
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, whichever way you arrive at it, it's going to require work of going against the grain of your existence, of your... because that's the nature of the trap.

328
00:24:09,857 --> 00:24:11,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Slide in effortlessly.

329
00:24:11,972 --> 00:24:15,995
[SPEAKER_01]: If a trap requires you work to get in, nobody would get trapped.

330
00:24:17,757 --> 00:24:22,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, you know, you want to catch a mouse, you put peanut butter in that humane trap.

331
00:24:22,721 --> 00:24:23,761
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't just go in it.

332
00:24:24,022 --> 00:24:27,725
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, sometimes they're really really dumb or something, but even then...

333
00:24:28,533 --> 00:24:30,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trap needs to be effortless.

334
00:24:30,654 --> 00:24:32,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the effortless in this life?

335
00:24:32,274 --> 00:24:34,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of sensuality is effortless.

336
00:24:34,955 --> 00:24:36,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of aversion when you're angry, it's effortless.

337
00:24:37,236 --> 00:24:44,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of indolence, towards indolence, thinking too much, too painful, making that effort of grinding against the grain, it's...

338
00:24:45,238 --> 00:24:46,719
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go with least resistance.

339
00:24:46,779 --> 00:24:50,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I go with the grain.

340
00:24:50,260 --> 00:24:52,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, that's why you're blameworthy.

341
00:24:53,461 --> 00:24:54,961
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're subject to suffering.

342
00:24:56,121 --> 00:24:57,662
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're subject to saṃsara.

343
00:24:59,583 --> 00:25:05,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, because of the choices or the lack of them, that you might have experienced, I didn't have many opportunities.

344
00:25:05,867 --> 00:25:10,370
[SPEAKER_01]: You're alive, you're breathing, your senses are intact, you have more opportunity than you need.

345
00:25:14,633 --> 00:25:24,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only person who would say, but even then, the Buddha would say they're responsible, is the one, you know, born blind, deaf, undeveloped, incapable of rational thought.

346
00:25:24,900 --> 00:25:25,461
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then,

347
00:25:26,327 --> 00:25:27,647
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not born like that by chance.

348
00:25:27,968 --> 00:25:30,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, the Buddha says that you have to take it in faith.

349
00:25:30,148 --> 00:25:32,829
[SPEAKER_01]: They're born like that because of the previous things they've done.

350
00:25:35,850 --> 00:25:36,771
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see that, yeah.

351
00:25:37,191 --> 00:25:46,414
[SPEAKER_01]: In this life, you recognize that every choice you make, every intentions you act upon, the results will have to be bound with you.

352
00:25:47,054 --> 00:25:52,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, yeah, if I was born short, ugly, sick or something, it's probably the choices I was making in the past.

353
00:25:52,817 --> 00:25:53,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I have no memory of it.

354
00:25:55,487 --> 00:26:01,091
[SPEAKER_01]: But based on the principle that I can understand here and now, this is no different.

355
00:26:01,351 --> 00:26:01,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

356
00:26:03,433 --> 00:26:04,393
[SPEAKER_00]: It's probably my fault.

357
00:26:05,654 --> 00:26:05,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

358
00:26:05,934 --> 00:26:13,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even if you don't necessarily think it's my fault, doesn't matter what, like, what you were born with, what matters is what you do with it, in that sense.

359
00:26:13,480 --> 00:26:19,464
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're, like, you know, not as good as others around you, it's irrelevant.

360
00:26:19,864 --> 00:26:21,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things are not the measure.

361
00:26:21,766 --> 00:26:23,027
[SPEAKER_01]: The measure is what you do.

362
00:26:23,931 --> 00:26:29,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you keep perpetuating greed, aversion, delusion, or you don't perpetuate greed, aversion, delusion.

363
00:26:30,735 --> 00:26:38,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, just by being born as a human, you're already more than fortunate enough to deal with saṃsāra.

364
00:26:39,741 --> 00:26:40,681
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the best realm.

365
00:26:41,321 --> 00:26:50,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to deal with saṃsāra if you're born as an animal, and it's virtually impossible to deal with saṃsāra if you're born as a deva or higher, because it's just too pleasant.

366
00:26:50,587 --> 00:26:52,028
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no issues that you see.

367
00:26:53,608 --> 00:27:02,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, human realm is the perfect balance of pain and pleasure for you to start reflecting on both, which is a necessary basis for uprooting the whole saṃsāra.

368
00:27:03,794 --> 00:27:06,776
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to start taking responsibility for what you think, what you choose.

369
00:27:07,396 --> 00:27:14,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't, you're blaming the pain, or blaming the pleasure, for your craving towards pain, towards pleasure, or craving away from pain.

370
00:27:15,521 --> 00:27:17,502
[SPEAKER_01]: That's in the nātya, nātya boils down to that.

371
00:27:19,494 --> 00:27:26,378
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as there is a degree of avoiding responsibility, means your craving will be justified by how you feel.

372
00:27:26,418 --> 00:27:27,958
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no justification for craving.

373
00:27:28,519 --> 00:27:29,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feeling is not a new.

374
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:32,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is not a new.

375
00:27:32,801 --> 00:27:33,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not a new.

376
00:27:33,701 --> 00:27:34,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral feeling is not a new.

377
00:27:35,815 --> 00:27:42,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving towards pleasure, craving away from pain, craving basically ignoring the neutral, isn't it?

378
00:27:42,300 --> 00:27:45,642
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no linear connection between feeling resulting in craving.

379
00:27:45,822 --> 00:27:45,962
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

380
00:27:46,503 --> 00:27:49,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is the connection of feeling resulting in craving.

381
00:27:50,126 --> 00:27:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance of feeling means craving.

382
00:27:53,267 --> 00:27:56,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop ignoring the feeling, you will be incapable of craving.

383
00:27:56,390 --> 00:27:58,132
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you crave for pleasure.

384
00:27:58,913 --> 00:28:02,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people would say that because it's pleasant and that's what you do.

385
00:28:02,977 --> 00:28:03,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

386
00:28:03,757 --> 00:28:03,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

387
00:28:04,038 --> 00:28:11,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's... People who would say that are people who obviously haven't made the effort to understand the nature of pleasure or at least think about it.

388
00:28:11,825 --> 00:28:16,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, so yeah, I choose pleasure because it's pleasant, but what is the reason for valuing the pleasure?

389
00:28:16,690 --> 00:28:17,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's pleasant.

390
00:28:17,730 --> 00:28:17,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

391
00:28:18,050 --> 00:28:21,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the reason for me valuing that which is pleasant?

392
00:28:21,532 --> 00:28:22,872
[SPEAKER_01]: A pleasure is... well, so what?

393
00:28:23,172 --> 00:28:24,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is a reason in itself?

394
00:28:25,173 --> 00:28:26,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you will be completely unaware of it.

395
00:28:27,353 --> 00:28:30,474
[SPEAKER_01]: You will just be blindly... you see a light, you fly into it.

396
00:28:30,814 --> 00:28:31,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But no.

397
00:28:31,315 --> 00:28:32,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You have choices.

398
00:28:32,475 --> 00:28:34,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You have choices even on what pleasure you want.

399
00:28:35,616 --> 00:28:36,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are involved there.

400
00:28:36,776 --> 00:28:38,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say, oh, it's just because of pleasure.

401
00:28:38,397 --> 00:28:45,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if pleasure is automatically the reason for pleasure, it wouldn't require your thinking, choosing, acting, postponing to get more.

402
00:28:47,229 --> 00:28:52,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you do all of these reflections upon value of pleasure, if pleasure is a value in itself?

403
00:28:53,916 --> 00:28:57,560
[SPEAKER_01]: If pleasure is a value in itself, experiencing pleasure is self-contained fully.

404
00:28:58,181 --> 00:28:59,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you need more of it then?

405
00:29:01,312 --> 00:29:05,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not feeling more pleasure, by the way, before you commit to sensuality.

406
00:29:06,076 --> 00:29:10,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you're seeking more pleasure on account of the pleasure right here, right now.

407
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:15,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there must be some other reason involved between the pleasure you have and pleasure you want.

408
00:29:15,963 --> 00:29:20,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the pleasure you have is the reason for pleasure you want, you already have it.

409
00:29:21,127 --> 00:29:23,069
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you'll be craving free, sensuality free.

410
00:29:23,749 --> 00:29:25,411
[SPEAKER_01]: In feeling, there will be just the feeling.

411
00:29:25,951 --> 00:29:26,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't, isn't it?

412
00:29:27,853 --> 00:29:30,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, saying pleasure is the reason for wanting pleasure, it's incorrect.

413
00:29:35,054 --> 00:29:38,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose pleasure to be the reason for wanting more pleasure.

414
00:29:42,497 --> 00:29:43,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you choose that?

415
00:29:43,798 --> 00:29:44,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you value pleasure.

416
00:29:44,818 --> 00:29:45,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you value pleasure?

417
00:29:47,039 --> 00:29:49,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're completely ignorant of its perilous nature.

418
00:29:51,541 --> 00:29:54,222
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they say contemplate danger, don't be negligent.

419
00:29:56,870 --> 00:29:58,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is truly dangerous.

420
00:29:58,091 --> 00:30:05,537
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know that, but if you take what the Buddha said on faith, and then actually start contemplating it, you get to see it, and then you realize, oh my goodness, I dodged a bullet here.

421
00:30:06,898 --> 00:30:09,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the relief of attainments.

422
00:30:10,861 --> 00:30:17,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Like paid the debt, freed from the prison, crossed the desert, all the similarities and jhānas, similar.

423
00:30:18,468 --> 00:30:19,869
[SPEAKER_01]: The burden has been laid down.

424
00:30:20,109 --> 00:30:20,669
[SPEAKER_01]: The duty.

425
00:30:20,830 --> 00:30:21,550
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to do anything.

426
00:30:21,630 --> 00:30:22,211
[SPEAKER_01]: I am free now.

